1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,324
A fair coin. Not a claim about any one flip

2
00:00:04,424 --> 00:00:07,444
— no single flip is ever half a head.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:12,424
Flip it once and the proportion of heads is nought or one. Flip

4
00:00:12,524 --> 00:00:17,485
it again and again, and the proportion goes wherever the flips send it.

5
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:21,227
Keep going. It stops wandering, and it settles.

6
00:00:21,683 --> 00:00:25,960
That settling is the whole definition. A probability is

7
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:29,701
a long-run frequency — a statement about what a

8
00:00:29,801 --> 00:00:33,919
repeated process does, and never about the next flip.

9
00:00:36,216 --> 00:00:41,638
Now the same sequence, read two ways at once. Above, the surplus of

10
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:47,324
heads over tails. Below, the proportion. One set of flips feeds both.

11
00:00:47,916 --> 00:00:53,330
Watch the two readouts move in opposite directions. The surplus climbs and

12
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:57,800
never comes back. The error in the rate falls the whole way.

13
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:01,629
Neither is an accident. The surplus has an exact

14
00:01:01,729 --> 00:01:05,937
expected size, and the proportion's error is that same

15
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:09,128
quantity divided by the number of flips.

16
00:01:09,216 --> 00:01:14,912
Two to the one minus n, times n, times a binomial coefficient — which

17
00:01:15,012 --> 00:01:20,036
for any n worth plotting is the square root of two n over pi.

18
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:24,879
Root n in the numerator, n in the denominator. That is the

19
00:01:24,979 --> 00:01:31,145
whole reason a deficit is never repaid and the proportion converges anyway.

20
00:01:31,233 --> 00:01:35,079
There is no law of averages. Nothing is owed and

21
00:01:35,179 --> 00:01:39,437
nothing is repaid. The denominator does all the work.

22
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:47,277
Here is the belief worth killing. After a run of heads, is a tail due?

23
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:52,218
Two million flips, sorted by the run that came before

24
00:01:52,318 --> 00:01:56,406
each one. After a single head, after two, after six

25
00:01:56,506 --> 00:01:59,362
— how often is the next flip a head?

26
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:03,333
Every bar lands on one half. Nothing happens, and

27
00:02:03,433 --> 00:02:06,910
the fact that nothing happens is the result.

28
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,544
That is independence. The sequence carries no debt, and it

29
00:02:11,644 --> 00:02:16,188
is also the assumption every control limit later rests on.

30
00:02:18,483 --> 00:02:22,175
One more idea before the chart. A fair die:

31
00:02:22,275 --> 00:02:25,703
six faces, each as likely as the others.

32
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:30,512
Slide a fulcrum until the six weights balance. It settles

33
00:02:30,612 --> 00:02:33,996
between three and four — at three point five.

34
00:02:34,083 --> 00:02:37,799
Three point five is not a face. The die can never

35
00:02:37,899 --> 00:02:41,303
show it, and it is still the value to expect.

36
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,710
An expected value is a balance point, not a

37
00:02:44,810 --> 00:02:48,596
prediction, and it need not be attainable at all.

38
00:02:50,883 --> 00:02:53,663
Level 6 will put a number on a pair of

39
00:02:53,763 --> 00:02:58,591
limits: nought point two seven percent outside. Three readings of

40
00:02:58,691 --> 00:03:01,775
that number, and only one of them is true.

41
00:03:01,866 --> 00:03:05,793
Not the part — a part is never a probability. Not the

42
00:03:05,893 --> 00:03:10,351
parts out of tolerance — that is capability, and it is Level

43
00:03:10,451 --> 00:03:13,694
8. It is a rate at which a chart cries wolf.

44
00:03:15,183 --> 00:03:18,864
So run the chart on a process where nothing is wrong,

45
00:03:18,964 --> 00:03:23,003
and count the chance of having been alarmed at least once.

46
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:29,020
A shift of a hundred subgroups, and it is already 24 percent.

47
00:03:29,116 --> 00:03:32,783
Keep plotting. The phrase is one alarm in three

48
00:03:32,883 --> 00:03:37,272
hundred and seventy subgroups, so run exactly that many.

49
00:03:37,366 --> 00:03:42,666
Not certainty — 63 percent. And that is not a coincidence.

50
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:46,378
Because the number of subgroups is one over the rate,

51
00:03:46,478 --> 00:03:49,826
the expression collapses to one minus one over e.

52
00:03:49,916 --> 00:03:54,518
And because the waiting time is geometric, the typical

53
00:03:54,618 --> 00:03:58,435
wait is shorter than the average one: half of

54
00:03:58,535 --> 00:04:02,440
all first false alarms arrive by subgroup 256.

55
00:04:02,533 --> 00:04:07,196
A percentage on a control chart is a claim about a repeated

56
00:04:07,296 --> 00:04:12,201
process. Never about a part. Level 6 can price the limits now.

